      link ¶ #property
    text Return a property attribute.
    text fget is a function for getting an attribute value. fset is a function for setting an attribute value. fdel is a function for deleting an attribute value. And doc creates a docstring for the attribute.
    text A typical use is to define a managed attribute x:
    text "class C: def __init__(self): self._x = None def getx(self): return self._x def setx(self, value): self._x = value def delx(self): del self._x x = property(getx, setx, delx, \"I'm the 'x' property.\")"
    text If c is an instance of C, c.x will invoke the getter, c.x = value will invoke the setter, and del c.x the deleter.
    text doc
    text fget
    link "@property" #property
    link decorator ../glossary.html#term-decorator
    text "class Parrot: def __init__(self): self._voltage = 100000 @property def voltage(self): \"\"\"Get the current voltage.\"\"\" return self._voltage"
    text The @property decorator turns the voltage() method into a “getter” for a read-only attribute with the same name, and it sets the docstring for voltage to “Get the current voltage.”
    row
      text @
      text getter
      link ¶ #property.getter
