      text zip
      text (
      text *iterables
      text strict=False
      text )
      link ¶ #zip
    text Iterate over several iterables in parallel, producing tuples with an item from each one.
    text Example:
    text >>> for item in zip([1, 2, 3], ['sugar', 'spice', 'everything nice']): ... print(item) ... (1, 'sugar') (2, 'spice') (3, 'everything nice')
    text More formally: zip() returns an iterator of tuples, where the i-th tuple contains the i-th element from each of the argument iterables.
    text zip()
    link "transposing a matrix" https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transpose
    text zip()
    text for
    link list stdtypes.html#list
    text One thing to consider is that the iterables passed to zip() could have different lengths; sometimes by design, and sometimes because of a bug in the code that prepared these iterables. Python offers three different approaches to dealing with this issue:
    row
      text zip()
      link exhausted ../glossary.html#term-exhausted
    text >>> list(zip(range(3), ['fee', 'fi', 'fo', 'fum'])) [(0, 'fee'), (1, 'fi'), (2, 'fo')]
    text zip() is often used in cases where the iterables are assumed to be of equal length. In such cases, it’s recommended to use the strict=True option. Its output is the same as regular zip():
    text >>> list(zip(('a', 'b', 'c'), (1, 2, 3), strict=True)) [('a', 1), ('b', 2), ('c', 3)]
    row
      link ValueError exceptions.html#ValueError
      link exhausted ../glossary.html#term-exhausted
    text >>> for item in zip(range(3), ['fee', 'fi', 'fo', 'fum'], strict=True): ... print(item) ... (0, 'fee') (1, 'fi') (2, 'fo') Traceback (most recent call last): ... ValueError: zip() argument 2 is longer than argument 1
    text Without the strict=True argument, any bug that results in iterables of different lengths will be silenced, possibly manifesting as a hard-to-find bug in another part of the program.
    link itertools.zip_longest() ../library/itertools.html#itertools.zip_longest
    text Edge cases: With a single iterable argument, zip() returns an iterator of 1-tuples. With no arguments, it returns an empty iterator.
    text Tips and tricks:
    text The left-to-right evaluation order of the iterables is guaranteed. This makes possible an idiom for clustering a data series into n-length groups using zip(*[iter(s)]*n, strict=True). This repeats the same iterator n times so that each output tuple has the result of n calls to the iterator. This has the effect of dividing the input into n-length chunks.
    text zip() in conjunction with the * operator can be used to unzip a list:
    text >>> x = [1, 2, 3] >>> y = [4, 5, 6] >>> list(zip(x, y)) [(1, 4), (2, 5), (3, 6)] >>> x2, y2 = zip(*zip(x, y)) >>> x == list(x2) and y == list(y2) True
    text Changed in version 3.10: Added the strict argument.
